# THE SIFREI ROWS OF PISKA 32 (THE DEUTERONOMY WALK sitting 4 — CHAPTER 6, 2026-09-17; run 2): the piska on 6:5 — "and you shall love" — twenty-one
# rows in both files, the long one: the love's motive (1-2), the three terms heart / soul / might (3-7), the chastisement digression (8-18), the three
# fathers for the three terms (19-21). ONE ROW CREDITED (32:2, read at gen_27 — Abraham's converts) with a quick look here. The cuts by ch6_ink.SP_;
# the middah named where the row argues by one (logic/MIDDOT.md). Speed ruling (c): CONTEXT rows terse. Sitting 3's form.
from ch6_ink import *
S = {}
def srow(p, r, verdict, prose): S[(p, r)] = (verdict, prose)
FEAR = sum(1 for v in range(1, 26) for l in byl[('Deut', 6, v)] if l.split()[0] == '3372')   # the lemma "fear" in the chapter, computed

srow(32, 1, 'MATERIAL', f'"AND YOU SHALL LOVE THE LORD YOUR GOD" (6:5) — DO FROM LOVE. SCRIPTURE DISTINGUISHES ONE WHO DOES FROM LOVE FROM ONE WHO DOES FROM FEAR: THE LOVER\'S REWARD IS DOUBLED AND REDOUBLED; "THE LORD YOUR GOD YOU SHALL FEAR AND HIM YOU SHALL SERVE" (10:20). A MAN WHO FEARS HIS FELLOW LEAVES HIM WHEN HE IS NEEDED — BUT YOU, DO FROM LOVE; THERE IS NO LOVE WHERE THERE IS FEAR NOR FEAR WHERE THERE IS LOVE, SAVE IN THE MEASURE OF THE OMNIPRESENT. The head row of piska 32 (on 6:5; {SP_(32, 1, "הפריש", "בין", "העושה", "מאהבה", "לעושה", "מיראה")} "He distinguished between one who does from love and one who does from fear"): the verse\'s verb made the MOTIVE of every deed, weighed against the chapter\'s own "fear" — the lemma at {FEAR} tokens of the chapter (6:2, 6:13, 6:24 — computed) — and 10:20\'s pair, the proof-text the twin of 6:13 (the diff: 10:20 adds "and to Him you shall cleave" — computed). The ink: "and you shall love the LORD your God" 6:5 and 11:1 (computed); "and you shall love" six seats over the Bible — the neighbor and the stranger at Leviticus 19:18, 34 among them (computed). The middah: a command read as a distinction between two doers (I12\'s kin — the meaning from the passage\'s pairing of love and fear). Fresh in both files.')
srow(32, 3, 'MATERIAL', f'"WITH ALL YOUR HEART" — WITH YOUR TWO INCLINATIONS, THE GOOD AND THE EVIL. The doubled letter read ({SP_(32, 3, "בשני", "יצריך", "ביצר", "טוב", "וביצר", "רע")} "with your two inclinations, with the good inclination and the evil"): לבבך ("your heart") with its doubled bet, against the shorter form — the case row Mishnah Berakhot 9:5 (the English\'s note; the answer sheet\'s). The ink: the chapter writes the long form at {[v for v in range(1, 26) if "לבבך" in W6(v)]} (computed on the tokens); the plural "your hearts" 11:13. The middah: a spelling read as a count (E10\'s kin — the doubled letter signifies two). Fresh in both files.')
srow(32, 4, 'MATERIAL', f'ANOTHER WORD: "WITH ALL YOUR HEART" — WITH ALL THE HEART IN YOU, THAT YOUR HEART BE NOT DIVIDED AGAINST THE OMNIPRESENT. A second reading of the same word ({SP_(32, 4, "שלא", "יהיה", "לבך", "חלוק", "על", "המקום")} "that your heart be not divided against the Omnipresent"): לבבך ("your heart") cut as לב בך ("a heart in you") — the undivided heart, 31:6\'s "your heart is not divided" from the other side. The middah: a word divided in two (E30 notarikon\'s form). Fresh in both files.')
srow(32, 5, 'MATERIAL', f'"AND WITH ALL YOUR SOUL" — EVEN IF HE TAKES YOUR SOUL; "FOR YOUR SAKE WE ARE KILLED ALL THE DAY" (PSALM 44:23); R. SHIMON BEN MENASYA: HOW CAN A MAN BE KILLED EVERY DAY? THE HOLY ONE COUNTS IT TO THE RIGHTEOUS AS IF KILLED EVERY DAY. BEN AZZAI: "WITH ALL YOUR SOUL" — LOVE HIM UNTIL THE SOUL IS WRUNG OUT. The martyr\'s reading ({SP_(32, 5, "אפלו", "הוא", "נוטל", "את", "נפשך")} "even if He takes your soul"): "soul" = the life itself, the love\'s limit set at death — Mishnah Berakhot 9:5 again (the English\'s note), Sanhedrin 74a\'s seat for the sins one dies for (the design\'s docket). The ink: "with all your heart and with all your soul" seven seats, all Deuteronomy\'s, 4:29 the first (computed). The middah: the word\'s widest sense taken ("all" the extension — E1\'s kin). Fresh in both files.')
srow(32, 6, 'MATERIAL', f'R. ELIEZER: IF "WITH ALL YOUR SOUL" IS SAID, WHY "WITH ALL YOUR MIGHT"? AND IF "MIGHT", WHY "SOUL"? THERE IS A MAN WHOSE BODY IS DEARER THAN HIS MONEY — FOR HIM "WITH ALL YOUR SOUL"; A MAN WHOSE MONEY IS DEARER THAN HIS BODY — FOR HIM "WITH ALL YOUR MIGHT". The two terms made two men ({SP_(32, 6, "אם", "נאמר", "בכל", "נפשך", "למה", "נאמר", "בכל", "מאדך")} "if \'with all your soul\' is said, why is \'with all your might\' said"): מאדך ("your might") read as MONEY — Mishnah Berakhot 9:5\'s "with all your money" (the answer sheet\'s), and ONKELOS\'S OWN RENDERING "with all your property" (the Onkelos row 6:5 below; נכסך "your property" ONE SEAT in the book — computed). The ink: מאדך THE BIBLE\'S ONE SEAT of the noun with a suffix (computed). The middah: two terms each given a case so neither is idle (E10 — repetition signifies; the "why is it said" form). Fresh in both files.')
srow(32, 7, 'MATERIAL', f'R. AKIVA: IF "WITH ALL YOUR SOUL", THEN A FORTIORI "WITH ALL YOUR MIGHT" — SO WHY SAY IT? WITH EVERY MEASURE THAT HE MEASURES OUT TO YOU, WHETHER THE MEASURE OF GOOD OR THE MEASURE OF PUNISHMENT — SO DAVID (PSALM 116:13, 116:3-4), SO JOB ("THE LORD GAVE AND THE LORD TOOK", 1:21; HIS WIFE 2:9 AND HIS ANSWER 2:10); THE GENERATION OF THE FLOOD ACCEPTED PUNISHMENT — HOW MUCH MORE WE. The word-play reading ({SP_(32, 7, "קל", "וחמר")} "an a-fortiori"; {SP_(32, 7, "בכל", "מדה", "ומדה")} "with every measure"): מאדך ("your might") heard as מדה ("measure") — the love owed under every measure, good or ill (the English\'s note: the assonance); the a-fortiori refused as making the word idle, then a second a-fortiori from the flood\'s generation. The middot: I1 stated and set aside (the word would be idle), then I1 again from the flood; a word read by its sound (the tradition\'s own play, outside the numbered rules); the Mekhilta Bahodesh 10 the parallel (the English\'s note). Fresh in both files.')
srow(32, 8, 'CONTEXT', f'AND FURTHER: LET A MAN REJOICE IN CHASTISEMENTS MORE THAN IN GOOD — GOOD ALL ONE\'S DAYS FORGIVES NO SIN; CHASTISEMENTS FORGIVE. The chastisement digression opens ({SP_(32, 8, "יהא", "אדם", "שמח", "ביסורים")} "let a man rejoice in chastisements") — 32:8-18 leave 6:5 for the measure of punishment. Fresh.')
srow(32, 9, 'CONTEXT', f'R. ELIEZER BEN JACOB: "WHOM THE LORD LOVES HE REBUKES, AS A FATHER THE SON HE FAVORS" (PROVERBS 3:12) — WHAT MADE THE SON FAVORED? CHASTISEMENTS. ({SP_(32, 9, "מי", "גרם", "לבן")} "what caused the son"). Fresh.')
srow(32, 10, 'CONTEXT', f'R. MEIR: "AS A MAN CHASTENS HIS SON THE LORD YOUR GOD CHASTENS YOU" (8:5) — YOU KNOW YOUR DEEDS AND THE CHASTISEMENTS, AND THE LATTER ARE NOT AS THE DEEDS. ({SP_(32, 10, "וידעת", "עם", "לבבך")} "and you shall know with your heart" — 8:5\'s "your heart", the chapter\'s word). Fresh.')
srow(32, 11, 'CONTEXT', f'R. YOSE SON OF R. JUDAH: BELOVED ARE CHASTISEMENTS — THE NAME OF THE OMNIPRESENT RESTS ON HIM WHO SUFFERS THEM (8:5). ({SP_(32, 11, "חביבים", "יסורים")} "beloved are chastisements" — the formula of 32:11, 13, 17). Fresh.')
srow(32, 12, 'CONTEXT', f'R. NATHAN SON OF R. JOSEPH: AS A COVENANT IS CUT FOR THE LAND, SO FOR CHASTISEMENTS (8:5, THEN 8:7). ({SP_(32, 12, "ברית", "כרותה", "ליסורים")} "a covenant is cut for chastisements"). Fresh.')
srow(32, 13, 'CONTEXT', f'R. SHIMON BEN YOHAI: BELOVED ARE CHASTISEMENTS — THREE GOOD GIFTS GIVEN TO ISRAEL THAT THE NATIONS COVET, GIVEN ONLY THROUGH CHASTISEMENTS: TORAH, THE LAND, THE WORLD TO COME. ({SP_(32, 13, "ששלש", "מתנות", "טובות")} "that three good gifts"). Fresh.')
srow(32, 14, 'CONTEXT', f'TORAH — WHENCE? PROVERBS 1:2 "WISDOM AND DISCIPLINE"; PSALM 94:12 "WHOM YOU CHASTEN … AND TEACH FROM YOUR TORAH". ({SP_(32, 14, "תורה", "מנין")} "Torah — whence?"). Fresh.')
srow(32, 15, 'CONTEXT', f'THE LAND — WHENCE? 8:5-7 "CHASTENS YOU … BRINGS YOU INTO A GOOD LAND". ({SP_(32, 15, "ארץ", "ישראל", "מנין")} "the land of Israel — whence?"). Fresh.')
srow(32, 16, 'CONTEXT', f'THE WORLD TO COME — WHENCE? PROVERBS 6:23 "THE WAY OF LIFE IS THE REBUKE OF DISCIPLINE". ({SP_(32, 16, "העולם", "הבא", "מנין")} "the world to come — whence?"). Fresh.')
srow(32, 17, 'CONTEXT', f'R. NEHEMIAH: AS OFFERINGS APPEASE SO CHASTISEMENTS APPEASE (LEVITICUS 1:4; 26:43) — AND MORE, FOR OFFERINGS COST MONEY AND CHASTISEMENTS THE BODY (JOB 2:4). ({SP_(32, 17, "שקרבנות", "מרצים", "כך", "יסורים", "מרצים")} "as offerings appease, so chastisements appease"). Fresh.')
srow(32, 18, 'CONTEXT', f'R. ELIEZER LAY SICK; R. TARFON, R. JOSHUA, R. ELAZAR BEN AZARIAH AND R. AKIVA VISITED — DEARER THAN THE SUN, THAN RAIN, THAN FATHER AND MOTHER; R. AKIVA: BELOVED ARE CHASTISEMENTS — MANASSEH (2 CHRONICLES 33:1, 33:11-13) LEARNED NOTHING FROM HEZEKIAH\'S TEACHING BUT FROM HIS CHAINS. The sickbed story ({SP_(32, 18, "וכבר", "היה", "רבי", "אליעזר", "חולה")} "and once R. Eliezer was sick"); Sanhedrin 101a-b the parallel. Fresh.')
srow(32, 19, 'MATERIAL', f'R. MEIR: "AND YOU SHALL LOVE THE LORD YOUR GOD WITH ALL YOUR HEART" — LOVE HIM WITH ALL YOUR HEART LIKE ABRAHAM YOUR FATHER ("THE SEED OF ABRAHAM MY FRIEND", ISAIAH 41:8). The three terms given the three fathers, 32:19-21 ({SP_(32, 19, "אהבהו", "בכל", "לבבך", "כאברהם", "אביך")} "love Him with all your heart like Abraham your father"): the heart Abraham\'s — the lover named by the prophet. Fresh in both files.')
srow(32, 20, 'MATERIAL', f'"WITH ALL YOUR SOUL" — LIKE ISAAC, WHO BOUND HIMSELF ON THE ALTAR (GENESIS 22:10). ({SP_(32, 20, "כיצחק", "שעקד", "עצמו")} "like Isaac who bound himself"): the soul Isaac\'s — 32:5\'s "even if He takes your soul" given its father. Fresh in both files.')
srow(32, 21, 'MATERIAL', f'"WITH ALL YOUR MIGHT" — BE THANKFUL TO HIM LIKE JACOB YOUR FATHER: "I AM TOO SMALL FOR ALL THE KINDNESSES … WITH MY STAFF I CROSSED THIS JORDAN AND NOW I AM TWO CAMPS" (GENESIS 32:11). The piska\'s close ({SP_(32, 21, "הוי", "מודה", "לו", "כיעקב", "אביך")} "be thankful to Him like Jacob your father"): מאדך ("your might") heard as מודה ("thankful") — the third reading of the one-seat word after money (32:6) and measure (32:7); the three fathers the three terms. The middah: the word read by its sound (as 32:7). Fresh in both files.')

# THE CREDITED ROW (read at gen_27 — named in the ledger's spine section with a quick look, not read fresh here)
CRED = {(32, 2): f'ANOTHER WORD: "AND YOU SHALL LOVE THE LORD YOUR GOD" — MAKE HIM BELOVED UPON THE CREATURES LIKE ABRAHAM YOUR FATHER ("THE SOULS THEY HAD MADE IN HARAN", GENESIS 12:5 — ALL THE WORLD TOGETHER COULD NOT MAKE ONE GNAT AND PUT BREATH IN IT: ABRAHAM BROUGHT THEM UNDER THE WINGS OF THE PRESENCE). Read at gen_27 (its row 50, a duplicate of its row 46 — the Genesis seat 12:5); the quick look for THIS chapter\'s op ({SP_(32, 2, "אהבהו", "על", "הבריות")} "make Him beloved upon the creatures"): "love" read TRANSITIVELY OUTWARD — the lover makes others love; the standing row read it for Abraham\'s converts, and the same reading serves 6:5. Credited.'}

assert sorted(S) + sorted(CRED) == sorted((32, r) for r in range(1, SPINE_ROWS[32] + 1)) or sorted(list(S) + list(CRED)) == [(32, r) for r in range(1, SPINE_ROWS[32] + 1)], sorted(list(S) + list(CRED))
